// What a colony calls home. A plain `Solid` -- deliberately the simplest
// thing that renders distinctly and that an ant can stand next to, because
// the nest in v1 is a *sensed material*, not a structure with behaviour.
// `AtNest` is a contact scan for this material and nothing else.
//
// The whole "find your way home" mechanism is elsewhere and is not about
// this file: an ant's channel-A deposit is scaled by how recently it
// touched nest material, so outbound ants paint a gradient that is
// freshest nearest home, and a laden ant walks up it. No ant ever asks
// where the nest is. This material only has to be recognisable on contact.
//
// **It has no colour of its own, and that is the point.** Owner playtest,
// 2026-09-14: *"when I found ants there are little yellow bars that get placed
// onto the ground. I don't like this."* Rated 1 of 5 against a first attempt
// that only changed the patch's *shape*: *"None. There should be no color. If
// we have to have this, it should be invisible."*
//
// So the palette below is `soil.ron`'s, entry for entry and family for family,
// and `World::paint_nest_patch` hands each new cell the **shade byte of the
// cell it replaced** rather than drawing a fresh one. `cell_colour` resolves a
// cell as `palette[shade % len]`, so a nest cell painted over loam draws the
// loam it covered -- the same family, the same tone, the same grain -- rather
// than something chosen to be near it.
//
// The old palette was three tones of pale warm tan, authored "to read clearly
// against soil and against the dark ants standing on it". That is a real want
// and it belongs to the *lab*, which is a diagnostic box; it is not a thing
// the held world's player ever asked to see. The two games share this file and
// only one of them has a view on it, so the one with the complaint wins and
// the lab loses a cue it can get back through an overlay if it wants one.
//
// **What this does not reach**: `cell_colour` darkens ground by held water,
// gated on `water_capacity`, and only `soil.ron` opts in. A nest cell
// therefore still draws *dry* on wet ground. Giving this material a capacity
// is a recorded dead end and must not be retried -- on a `Solid`, `Cell::aux`
// is the structural anchor distance, so the field would be read as moisture
// and painted as dampness. The fix, if the wet case matters, is render-side.
// See `Reports/lanes/druid-founding.md` for the measurement.
(
    name: "nest",
    display: "Nest",
    kind: Solid,
    density: 2.0,
    // `soil.ron`'s list, unchanged: three families of four, reference loam
    // then wet then dry, and `base_colors` keeps a random draw inside the
    // first family exactly as it does there. Copied rather than referenced
    // because the format has no include; the guard
    // `the_nest_draws_in_the_grounds_own_colours` reads both files and fails
    // if they ever part.
    colors: [
        // 0: reference loam
        (64, 46, 34),
        (74, 54, 40),
        (56, 40, 30),
        (82, 60, 44),
        // 1: wet, dark and rich
        (52, 36, 24),
        (60, 43, 29),
        (44, 30, 20),
        (68, 49, 33),
        // 2: dry, pale and dusty
        (96, 80, 62),
        (108, 91, 71),
        (84, 70, 54),
        (118, 100, 78),
    ],
    base_colors: 4,
    penetration_resistance: 6.0,
    heat_conductivity: 0.3,
)
